          'ELT-Bench was the first serious exam for AI agents building complete pipelines: 100 pipelines, 835 source tables, 203 data models. In April 2025, the best agent scored 57% on extract and load, and 3.9% on transform. A year later, a team from IBM Research and ETH Zurich re-ran it with current models: 96–98% and 32.5%. The gap closes from the bottom up — the mechanical first, the semantic last. And the exam\'s own audit surfaces the number that reframes everything: interpreting the same written specification, experts agreed only 57.8% of the time. This piece argues that transform doesn\'t resist because the model is limited, but because the business rule was never written unambiguously — and that every hour AI saves on mechanical work has to be reinvested there.',
